# Host-side upsmon (SECONDARY) — managed by Ansible (nut_host role); edits overwritten.
#
# The NUT primary lives in the k8s `nut` pod (USB access); it raises FSD on battery
# events but cannot power off the host. This secondary is the one that actually shuts
# the host down when FSD is set.
#
# The endpoint differs by host, which is why it is templated. The UPS host reaches upsd
# over the pod's loopback hostPort (127.0.0.1:3493). Any other host has to cross nodes
# and uses the ClusterIP, resolved at deploy time — the role's defaults explain why the
# LAN publish is not an option and why no NetworkPolicy change is needed for this.
MONITOR {{ nut_ups_name | default('apc-ups') }}@{{ nut_host_upsd_host }} 1 upsmon {{ nut_monitor_password }} secondary
MINSUPPLIES 1
SHUTDOWNCMD "/usr/bin/systemctl poweroff"
POLLFREQ 5
POLLFREQALERT 5
HOSTSYNC 15
DEADTIME 15
POWERDOWNFLAG /etc/killpower

NOTIFYMSG ONBATT "UPS %s on battery (host secondary)"
NOTIFYMSG LOWBATT "UPS %s battery is low (host secondary)"
NOTIFYMSG SHUTDOWN "Host auto-shutdown initiated by UPS %s"

NOTIFYFLAG ONBATT SYSLOG
NOTIFYFLAG LOWBATT SYSLOG
NOTIFYFLAG SHUTDOWN SYSLOG+WALL

RBWARNTIME 43200
NOCOMMWARNTIME 300
# DECIDED: FINALDELAY stays 5 on every host, and is deliberately NOT parameterised to stagger
# the two poweroffs. The tempting design is to let daniel-box shut down before daniel-server,
# whose pod hosts upsd, so it cannot be stranded when upsd disappears. It is unnecessary: a
# secondary needs upsd only to OBSERVE FSD, not to complete SHUTDOWNCMD, and both hosts observe
# it within one POLLFREQALERT (5 s) of the pod's `upsmon -c fsd` — long before daniel-server's
# poweroff reaches containerd. Staggering would also rest on FINALDELAY applying in secondary
# mode, which upsmon.conf(5) documents for primary mode only. Full reasoning in
# roles/k8s/nut/CLAUDE.md.
FINALDELAY 5
